From b6f57b0a98a3f0e4b412a4f737c44734a106bea4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tawheed Makhdoomi <_@tawheed.cc>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:51:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 30/41] media: qcom: camss: sm8150: don't drop shared camera
 clocks to 19.2 MHz

The SM8150 CSID resources list vfe*_cphy_rx, vfe* and vfe*_axi with a
rate of { 0 }. csid_set_clock_rates() applies freq[0] to such clocks, so
every CSID power-up calls clk_set_rate(clk, 0), which rounds to the
lowest supported rate (19.2 MHz):

 - cphy_rx_clk_src also feeds csiphyN_clk, so the running CSIPHY drops
   from 400 MHz to 19.2 MHz;
 - vfe*_axi propagates to camnoc_axi_clk_src, so the camera NoC runs at
   19.2 MHz.

On the Surface Pro X (SC8180X) this hangs the SoC on the next CSIPHY
register access (stream start or stop) and starves the display. The VFE
resources have the same problem for cpas_ahb/camnoc_axi (first entry
19.2 MHz) and vfe*_axi ({ 0 }).

Use fixed rates like SM8250 does: CPHY RX, VFE (from the CSID side) and
camnoc AXI 400 MHz, CPAS AHB 80 MHz.

